    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Few garden details give the same structure and precision as steel edging. Whether you want a minimalist modern border or a rustic, weathered look, steel holds a crisp line that plastic and timber struggle to match. That quality comes with a higher starting cost than most alternatives, and understanding it means looking beyond the price of the strip itself: the finish, the thickness, the installation and how long the edging will keep its shape all play a part.</p>
<p>Prices vary widely by supplier, region and year, so rather than quote figures that will soon be out of date, this guide explains what makes one steel edging job cost more than another, how to get quotes you can genuinely compare, and where you can save without ending up with a wavy, rusting border.</p>

<h2 id="untreated-steel">1. Untreated steel</h2>
<p>Untreated or "raw" mild (carbon) steel is usually the most budget-friendly way into metal edging. It is sold uncoated and is meant to oxidise, turning from a dark grey to a rusty brown. Because there is no coating to apply, it is generally the cheapest steel to make.</p>
<p>It suits gardens where you want the strength of steel on a tighter budget and like the look of natural rust. The catch is that ordinary mild steel is not weathering steel: it does not form a stable protective layer, so it will keep rusting and gradually thin over time. How quickly depends on the thickness, your soil and how wet the border stays, and acidic or salty soil speeds it up. Thicker raw steel buys you more time, which is one reason the cheapest product is not always the best value.</p>

<h2 id="galvanised-steel">2. Galvanised steel</h2>
<p>Galvanised steel has a zinc coating that protects it from rust and corrosion. It is a popular choice for damp gardens and for anyone who prefers a clean, silver-grey metal look that stays consistent rather than turning orange.</p>
<p>It typically costs more than raw steel because of the extra coating process. In return, it resists rust far better as long as the coating stays intact, so it suits contemporary designs where a rusted look is not wanted. Cut ends and deep scratches expose bare steel, so they need treating with a suitable zinc-rich paint, and the coating's durability varies between products; check the manufacturer's warranty for its stated term.</p>

<!--figure:galvanised-steel-trough-planter|Galvanised steel has a zinc coating that keeps rust away for years.-->

<h2 id="weathering-steel">3. Weathering steel</h2>
<p>Weathering steel is an alloy designed to develop a stable, rust-coloured surface layer (patina) that slows further corrosion. Unlike raw mild steel, which can flake and thin, its patina helps protect the metal underneath once it has formed.</p>
<p>It is often the choice for architectural, high-end gardens, and the price usually reflects that: expect it to cost noticeably more than raw or galvanised steel. For many gardens it is worth it, because it gives the warm rusted look with much better durability than untreated steel. Two things to plan for: the patina takes time to settle and needs wet and dry cycles to form properly, and run-off can stain nearby paving, pale gravel or render while it develops, so think about where water will drain. Durability still depends on conditions; constantly wet soil or salty coastal air can hold the patina back, so check the manufacturer's guidance and warranty for your situation.</p>

<!--figure:weathered-steel-with-planting|Weathering steel forms a stable rust-brown surface that suits planted gardens.-->

<h2 id="powder-coated-steel">4. Powder-coated steel</h2>
<p>Powder-coated edging is steel, often galvanised first, finished with a dry powder that is cured under heat to form a hard, durable skin. The usual colours are black, brown and dark green, so the edging can either disappear into the planting or frame a border as a deliberate design line.</p>
<p>It generally sits in the mid-to-upper part of the range, often above plain galvanised steel. It is a good fit if you want a particular colour that will not chip or fade as easily as paint. The finish is only as good as its weakest point, though: chips from tools or stones should be touched up with the manufacturer's recommended repair paint so rust cannot creep under the coating.</p>

<!--figure:black-powder-coated-planters|Powder coating adds colour and protection, usually in black or dark grey.-->

<h2 id="stainless-steel">5. Stainless steel</h2>
<p>Stainless steel is rare in home gardens and usually the most expensive edging option, because of the cost of the alloy. It is highly resistant to rust and gives a bright, polished, sophisticated look. It tends to be used in luxury schemes, around water features or in very formal designs where maintenance must be kept to a minimum. Grades differ in how well they cope with coastal or salty conditions, so ask the supplier which grade suits your site.</p>

<h2 id="what-drives-cost">6. What else drives steel edging cost</h2>
<p>The finish is only part of the picture. Several other factors can shift the final quote considerably.</p>

<h3>Thickness</h3>
<p>Steel thickness is sometimes quoted as a US gauge number, where a higher gauge means thinner metal, so it is clearer to compare products by their thickness in millimetres. Lighter strips, around 1.5 mm (roughly 16 gauge) or thinner, are flexible, easy to curve and usually the cheapest, but they can buckle under soil pressure and knocks. Many landscape-grade edgings are around 1.5–3 mm (roughly 16 to 11 gauge), with the heavier end giving a more rigid edge that copes better with gravel, heavier soil and busier areas. Very thick steel, around 6 mm (¼ in) or more, is usually sold as plate rather than strip and used for raised beds or retaining very low level changes; it costs considerably more and generally needs a bender for curves. Thicker steel uses more metal and weighs more to move and deliver, so the price rises with it.</p>

<h3>Height</h3>
<p>A standard edging strip is usually around 10 cm (4 in) high. If you need to hold back deeper roots, contain a thicker layer of gravel or create a more visible border, taller strips of around 15–20 cm (6–8 in) are available. Taller strips use proportionally more steel, so the price scales with the height as well as the thickness.</p>

<h3>Length, curves and corners</h3>
<p>Total length is the obvious factor, but the layout matters as much. Long straight runs are the cheapest to buy and fit. Tight curves may mean a thinner, more flexible product or more time bending heavier steel, and every corner, junction or step needs cutting, a corner piece or a connector. A design with lots of small curved beds can cost noticeably more than the same length laid as a few clean lines.</p>

<h3>Fixings, stakes and connectors</h3>
<p>Stakes, joining sleeves or tabs, corner pieces and end caps are sometimes included and sometimes sold separately, so check before comparing products. Loose or sandy soil may need longer stakes or more of them, and better connector systems usually cost a little more, but weak joints and too few stakes are among the most common reasons steel edging fails.</p>

<h3>Delivery</h3>
<p>Steel is heavy, and delivery is one of the most overlooked costs. A low price per length online can look less attractive once heavy-goods delivery is added at the checkout, particularly for long strips, thick steel or small orders. Compare the delivered price, and ask whether a local supplier or your installer can collect or combine the order with other materials.</p>

<h3>Labour</h3>
<p>Labour can easily make up a large part of the total, and it depends on local rates and the site. The things that push it up are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Site preparation:</strong> rocky ground, roots or heavy clay make trenching slower.</li>
<li><strong>Removing old edging:</strong> digging out old plastic, timber or rusted metal adds time and disposal.</li>
<li><strong>Curves and complexity:</strong> straight lines are quick; intricate curves, custom corners and steps need more precision.</li>
<li><strong>Cutting:</strong> each cut takes time, and thick steel takes longer to cut and finish.</li>
<li><strong>Access and size:</strong> narrow side passages slow the job down, while larger projects can work out cheaper per metre because set-up time is spread further.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="diy-vs-professional">7. DIY or professional installation</h2>
<p>Steel edging is one of the more DIY-friendly garden projects, especially systems with pre-attached or slot-in stakes. Your main costs are the materials, a few tools (a spade or half-moon edger, a rubber mallet, a block of wood to protect the top edge, and something to cut the steel) and your time.</p>
<p>Cutting is the part that needs care. Thin strips can be cut with a hacksaw, which is slower but safer and quieter. Thicker steel is usually cut with an angle grinder and a metal-cutting disc, which is quick but needs proper precautions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Wear eye protection, ear protection and sturdy gloves, and follow the tool manufacturer's instructions for the disc and guard.</li>
<li>Grinding throws hot sparks, so cut away from dry grass, mulch, fences, sheds and anything flammable, and keep water to hand.</li>
<li>Clamp the steel firmly rather than holding it by hand.</li>
<li>File off the burrs afterwards so the cut ends are not sharp, and treat cut ends on coated steel as the manufacturer recommends.</li>
</ul>
<p>A professional installer is worth considering for long runs, heavy or plate steel, tight curves, rocky or clay ground, or where the edging also has to retain soil. They will also usually have the right cutting and bending equipment, which saves you buying or hiring it.</p>

<h2 id="long-term-value">8. Long-term value compared with plastic</h2>
<p>Plastic edging is usually much cheaper to buy, but it can become brittle in sunlight, crack, and lift out of the ground with frost. Steel costs more at the start, and in return it holds a cleaner line, stands up better to mowers and strimmers and, installed well, typically outlasts plastic and timber. If cheaper edging would need replacing more than once over the time steel stays in the ground, steel can work out to be the better value overall. How long any edging lasts depends on the product, your soil, climate and care, so check the manufacturer's warranty for its stated term.</p>

<h2 id="comparing-quotes">9. How to get quotes you can compare</h2>
<p>Edging quotes are only comparable if they price the same job. Give two or three installers the same plan and specification, and ask for an itemised quote covering:</p>
<ul>
<li>The total length, split into straight runs and curved runs, and the number of corners and ends.</li>
<li>The product: finish, thickness in millimetres and height.</li>
<li>Stakes, connectors, corner pieces and end caps, including how many stakes and at what spacing.</li>
<li>Delivery of the materials.</li>
<li>Site preparation, trenching and backfilling, and any allowance for rocky or clay ground.</li>
<li>Removal and disposal of old edging, turf or spoil.</li>
<li>Cutting, bending and finishing cut ends.</li>
<li>Making good the lawn and beds afterwards, and any top-up of gravel or mulch.</li>
</ul>
<p>If one quote is much lower than the rest, check which of these items it leaves out, and whether it uses thinner or shorter steel.</p>

<h2 id="ways-to-save">10. Ways to keep the cost down</h2>
<ul>
<li>Simplify the layout: fewer, gentler curves and fewer corners mean less cutting, fewer connectors and less labour.</li>
<li>Use a premium finish such as weathering or powder-coated steel only where it is on show, and a simpler product along hidden or back borders.</li>
<li>Choose the height you actually need; a standard strip is enough for most lawn edges.</li>
<li>Do not save by going too thin. A strip that buckles will need replacing, which costs far more than a slightly heavier product at the start.</li>
<li>Order all the edging, stakes and connectors in one delivery, and measure carefully with a little spare for cuts to avoid a second delivery charge.</li>
<li>Do the easy parts yourself, such as lifting old edging or clearing the line, and leave cutting heavy steel and tricky curves to the installer.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask installers to price two thicknesses or finishes in the same quote. It is the easiest way to see exactly what an upgrade adds to your particular layout.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does steel edging rust through over time?</h3>
<p>Untreated mild steel will keep rusting and gradually thin, and how long it lasts depends on its thickness, your soil and how wet the border stays. Weathering steel is designed to form a stable surface layer that slows further corrosion, and galvanised or powder-coated steel relies on its coating, so keep chips and cut ends treated. Check the manufacturer's warranty for its stated term.</p>
<h3>Is steel edging dangerous for children or pets?</h3>
<p>Good landscape edging usually has a rolled, folded or rounded top edge to reduce the risk of cuts, but thin, cheap strips and freshly cut ends can be sharp. In family gardens, look for products with a safety edge, file cut ends smooth and keep the top close to soil level so it is not a trip hazard.</p>
<h3>Can I install steel edging on a slope?</h3>
<p>Steel edging works well on gentle slopes, staked firmly into the ground. For anything more than a small level change, edging strips are not designed to act as retaining walls; heavier plate steel or a proper retaining structure may be needed, and for larger level changes ask a landscaper or engineer.</p>
<h3>How do I stop steel edging heaving in winter?</h3>
<p>Bury it to the depth the manufacturer recommends, use stakes of the length and spacing they specify (longer ones in loose or sandy soil), put a stake at every joint and firm the soil well on both sides. Check it each spring and tap any lifted sections back down with a mallet and a block of wood.</p>
<h3>How do I cut steel edging if it is too long?</h3>
<p>An angle grinder with a metal-cutting disc is the quickest tool, used with eye and ear protection, gloves and care to keep sparks away from anything flammable. For thinner strips, a hacksaw works, though it takes more effort. File the cut end smooth and treat it if the steel is coated.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>The cost of steel edging comes down to the finish, the thickness and height, the length and shape of the layout, the fixings, delivery and labour. A slightly higher outlay on the right steel, properly fixed, usually pays off in a border that keeps its clean line for many seasons. Before you order, read up on the common <a href="/blog/steel-edging-mistakes">steel edging mistakes</a>, and once it is in, our guide to <a href="/blog/steel-edging-maintenance-by-season">steel edging maintenance by season</a> will help keep it looking sharp. If you are still choosing a material, compare the options in <a href="/blog/landscape-edging-pros-and-cons">landscape edging pros and cons</a>. For help designing a garden or terrace where every edge, path and border works together, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
